---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="Privacy &amp; Responsible Use · FloodWatch.PH" pathname="/privacy" description="How FloodWatch.PH handles province-level flood data, how it complies with RA 10173 (the Data Privacy Act), and how to request takedown.">
  <section class="max-w-prose mx-auto px-6 pt-10 pb-12">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-500 mb-3 font-mono">privacy &amp; responsible use</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl mb-5 leading-tight text-ink-900 tracking-tight">What FloodWatch.PH publishes, and what it doesn't.</h1>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      FloodWatch.PH is an open research project. All published data comes from public, openly licensed satellite and environmental sources, summed up by province only (a city and barangay breakdown is a later refinement). This page describes what we publish and what we don't, how it complies with RA 10173 (the Data Privacy Act of 2012), and how to request takedown.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-8 mb-3 text-ink-900">What we publish</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc list-outside ml-5 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Province totals for who and what is exposed (people and built-up land): population (population data (WorldPop), rounded to the nearest 10), built-up area in km&sup2; (building footprints (Microsoft)), the count of past floods on record (Global Flood Database), and the worst flood share (the largest fraction of the province under water across the demo dates). Whether each province is on an official hazard map is left blank in this version (that comparison is a later step).</li>
      <li>The mapped flood area as map files at the level of open water seen by satellite. These are environmental shapes, not home-level outlines.</li>
      <li>The prediction-vs-record bucket for each province.</li>
      <li>Track B (the flood-prediction model) sample points on a <strong>300 m</strong> grid, not buildings. No home identifiers.</li>
      <li>The trained model, the cached satellite fingerprints of the land, and the full chain anyone needs to re-run it and get the identical result.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-8 mb-3 text-ink-900">What we don't publish</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc list-outside ml-5 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Any home-level flood-status shape. Nothing that would let someone tell whether a specific house was flooded.</li>
      <li>Owner names, resident names, phone numbers, tax-declaration data, or anything tying a location to a person.</li>
      <li>Any personal-data field in any published map file. An automated check asserts this on every release.</li>
      <li>Any record of an area or route a user looks up. The lookup runs in your browser; FloodWatch receives nothing.</li>
      <li>Any new outbound user data from the Expressway watch layers. The added map-tile sources receive only fixed nationwide tile coordinates, never your lookup query.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      Population counts in the data file (filename kept for compatibility; this version's units are provinces) are rounded to the nearest 10. At province scale, individual household data cannot be worked back out.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-8 mb-3 text-ink-900">How it complies with RA 10173 (the Data Privacy Act)</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      FloodWatch.PH publishes environmental data summed up by province. Under §3(g) of RA 10173, personal information is "any information from which the identity of an individual is apparent or can be reasonably and directly ascertained." A satellite flood map at province scale, or a built-up-area figure for a province, does not identify a person. We address the "put together with other information" clause by withholding all sub-province shapes and any per-home status.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Environmental data carries far less privacy risk than personal data. A flood map and a prediction-vs-record bucket are findings about places, not records about people. The Privacy Impact Assessment at <a href="https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph/blob/main/docs/privacy-impact-assessment.md" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">docs/privacy-impact-assessment.md</a> documents this in detail.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-8 mb-3 text-ink-900">Data Protection Officer</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Xavier Puspus is the self-designated DPO for FloodWatch.PH. Contact at <a href="mailto:xpuspus@gmail.com?subject=DPO%20inquiry" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">xpuspus@gmail.com</a> (subject: <em>DPO inquiry</em>).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-8 mb-3 text-ink-900">Takedown channel</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      If you believe a published feature is incorrect, misleading, or presents a privacy concern, request takedown via:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc list-outside ml-5 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Open a <a href="https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph/issues/new?labels=takedown&amp;template=takedown.md" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">GitHub issue with the <code class="text-sm">takedown</code> label</a> (preferred, public audit trail).</li>
      <li>Email <a href="mailto:xpuspus@gmail.com?subject=Takedown%20request" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">xpuspus@gmail.com</a> with subject <em>Takedown request</em> and the province id or coordinates.</li>
      <li>For an active doxxing concern, use the GitHub Security advisory form: <a href="https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph/security/advisories/new" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">private advisory</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      Acknowledgement within 5 working days. Feature removed within 14 working days, with a public note in the next release log unless removal requires confidentiality.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-8 mb-3 text-ink-900">Public-records framing</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      All data inputs come from public records and public licensing: Copernicus Sentinel-1 (ESA/EC, open), Google AlphaEarth (CC-BY-4.0), Global Flood Database (Cloud to Street, CC-BY-4.0), MERIT Hydro (open), JRC Global Surface Water (open), population data (WorldPop) (CC-BY-4.0), building footprints (Microsoft) (ODbL-equivalent), province boundaries (public), OpenStreetMap (ODbL). Official hazard maps (Philippine government public records) are a later input, not used in this version.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      FloodWatch.PH computes statistical indicators derived from these public sources. Patterns shown may have legitimate explanations. The dataset does not constitute an official hazard map, an engineering assessment, or a code-compliance record, and should not be cited as such.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-8 mb-3 text-ink-900">Telemetry, cookies, analytics, and the area lookup</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      No first-party cookies. No analytics scripts. No user accounts. No advertising. Vercel sets standard CDN cookies for cache routing; we do not read or use them. The site is a static build. The <code>vercel.json</code> Content-Security-Policy explicitly enumerates which endpoints the browser can reach: <code>'self'</code>, OSM tile servers for the basemap, and OSM attribution endpoints. No third-party geocoder, no analytics endpoints, no tracking.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      The "is this area predicted to flood?" lookup runs <strong>entirely in your browser</strong> against a place-name list shipped with the site. The text you type is matched on your device and checked against the mapped flood area there too. Your query is <strong>never sent to any server, never logged, and never republished</strong>. It is your own input about your own area of interest; under RA 10173 it is not collected or processed by FloodWatch because it never leaves your device.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      The rainfall and expressway context view loads a few map-tile layers directly from public meteorological and government sources. These requests carry no information about what you searched: every visitor's browser fetches the same fixed nationwide tiles, and the area lookup still runs entirely in your browser and is never transmitted.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc list-outside ml-5 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1 mt-3">
      <li><strong>RainViewer (api.rainviewer.com, *.rainviewer.com, tilecache.rainviewer.com):</strong> ground-radar rain tiles relayed from PAGASA. The request contains no information about what you searched: the same map for every visitor.</li>
      <li><strong>NASA GIBS (gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov):</strong> NASA's near-real-time optical flood imagery tiles. The request contains no information about what you searched: the same map for every visitor.</li>
      <li><strong>EODC STAC / titiler (stac.eodc.eu, titiler.services.eodc.eu):</strong> the Copernicus EMS Global Flood Monitoring satellite flood-extent tile index and tile server. The request contains no information about what you searched: the same map for every visitor.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      None of these layers receive, see, or could infer the place or route you looked up. The lookup stays in-browser; these are read-only map tiles, identical bytes for every visitor.
    </p>

    <p class="text-[11px] text-ink-500 mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-ink-300/30">
      Observed flood extent derived from public satellite data (Copernicus Sentinel-1, AlphaEarth, GFD, MERIT Hydro, JRC GSW, WorldPop, Microsoft GlobalML Footprints, FAO GAUL). FloodWatch.PH computes statistical indicators only. Patterns may have legitimate explanations; figures warrant independent verification. Last reviewed 2026-05.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
